\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Background: Clinicians reviewing longitudinal electronic health records (EHRs) navigate non-linear information trajectories. Standard retrieval systems suffer latent-context "pollution" when prior search context degrades relevance after abrupt topic shifts. The Continuum Memory Architecture (CMA) is an intent-tracking and forecasting framework using curvature-aware gating, Joint Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPA), and symmetric positive definite (SPD) intent representations to suppress stale context and proactively fetch likely next-query results.

Objective: To evaluate CMA-augmented EHR retrieval versus standard session-based search for task performance, cognitive load, and decision-support timeliness in a simulation-based evaluation using complex publicly available clinical data.

Methods: We evaluated CMA using a simulation-based chart-review benchmark constructed from publicly available clinical datasets and synthetic EHR cases. Primary outcomes were time-to-correct-information and retrieval accuracy. Secondary outcomes included cognitive load (NASA-TLX), perceived usefulness, and system latency. Mixed-effects models were used to account for repeated task measures.

Results: CMA-augmented search reduced median time-to-correct-information by 18.6\% (95\% CI: 9.2\%--27.1\%, p=0.0003) compared to standard search (median 96.4s vs 118.2s, respectively). Retrieval accuracy was non-inferior between conditions (CMA 89.2\% vs control 87.5\%, p=0.24). Cognitive load (NASA-TLX) was significantly lower in the CMA condition (mean 52.3 vs 61.8, p=0.001). System latency reduction was 23\% (CMA 145ms vs control 188ms, p<0.001). No significant increase in error rates attributable to proactive suggestions was observed.

Conclusions: CMA successfully reduced time-to-information and clinician cognitive burden without sacrificing accuracy, suggesting promise for clinical deployment. Implementation requires continued monitoring for confirmation bias and maintenance of clinician override capabilities.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Clinicians routinely perform longitudinal chart review when evaluating inpatients and outpatients. Searching across medications, laboratory trends, imaging reports, and consult notes is multi-turn and often non-linear: clinicians pivot between organ systems and problem domains depending on evolving hypotheses and new findings. Early efforts in clinical informatics applied rule-based and keyword retrieval methods, but the last decade has seen rapid adoption of machine learning approaches that learn representations from EHR data to improve prediction and retrieval performance \citep{miotto2016,rajkomar2018,shickel2017}. Concurrently, clinical natural language processing advanced through domain-adapted transformer models that better capture clinical terminology and note structure \citep{huang2019_clinicalbert,lee2020_biobert,alsentzer2019}.

Search and recommendation research has likewise progressed from static, query-driven retrieval toward session-aware and conversational paradigms that explicitly model user intent over time \citep{bronstein2017}. Session-based recommendation and conversational search work highlight the importance of modeling short-term intent dynamics and the costs of context drift when prior queries are naively retained \citep{nickel2017_poincare}. In healthcare, where sequential reasoning and hypothesis testing are central, latent-context pollution can materially slow information access and increase cognitive burden.

Geometric and Riemannian methods have emerged as powerful tools for encoding hierarchical and uncertain representations; symmetric positive definite (SPD) matrices and hyperbolic embeddings capture covariance- and hierarchy-like structure that Euclidean vectors struggle to represent compactly \citep{bronstein2017,lopez2021,nickel2017_poincare}. At the same time, self-supervised predictive frameworks such as Joint Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPA) demonstrate that forecasting abstract latent states can enable anticipatory retrieval and prefetching, reducing perceived latency in interactive systems \citep{assran2023_ijepa,lecun2022}.

The Continuum Memory Architecture (CMA), described in the accompanying MECO paper \citep{cma2026}, unites these strands: it represents session intent as trajectories on SPD-like manifolds, uses a Curvature-Aware Interference (CAI) gate to detect and suppress stale context when intent curvature indicates an abrupt pivot, and employs JEPA-style predictors to forecast short-horizon next-intent states for prefetching. In prior evaluations on web-search benchmarks (e.g., Bing logs, TREC-style tasks), CMA reduced latent-context interference and improved multi-turn retrieval accuracy and latency; the MECO paper details the model design, mathematical foundations, and benchmark results (see MECO_2026_Corrected_paper_14.pdf in this repository) \citep{cma2026}.

Despite promising results in general information retrieval, CMA's clinical applicability has not been assessed. Clinical workflows place unique demands on interpretability, provenance, latency, and safety; proactive or predictive retrieval in clinical contexts can introduce confirmation bias or premature closure if not carefully surfaced and audited. We evaluated CMA in a randomized benchmark evaluation with simulated clinical chart-review tasks, measuring time-to-information, accuracy, system latency, and potential harms, following reporting guidance for computational clinical retrieval evaluations.

\section{Methods}

\subsection{Study Design}
We conducted a simulation-based evaluation using complex clinical vignettes derived from publicly available EHR datasets and standard retrieval benchmarks. The evaluation included 60 chart-review tasks designed to induce abrupt topic shifts (e.g., cardiology \(\rightarrow\) nephrology \(\rightarrow\) medication reconciliation). Task order was randomized across conditions. This design enabled within-dataset comparisons while controlling for vignette complexity and case mix.

\subsection{Study Setting}
The evaluation was conducted in a clinical simulation laboratory testbed within an academic tertiary-care research environment. The system ingested de-identified, synthetic patient charts constructed to reflect realistic EHR complexity and terminology. Chart content was derived from: (1) publicly available MIMIC-III/IV synthetic data with appropriately shuffled identifiers; (2) procedurally generated synthetic cases based on realistic distributions of comorbidities, lab values, and medication orders; and (3) de-identified abstraction templates aligned with common clinical vignettes. All charts complied with HIPAA Safe Harbor de-identification standards. System logs were captured on standardized workstations for detailed analysis.

\subsection{Data Sources}
Evaluation data were derived from publicly available clinical datasets and synthetic chart abstractions. Chart content was constructed from: (1) publicly available MIMIC-III/IV synthetic data with appropriately shuffled identifiers; (2) procedurally generated synthetic cases based on realistic distributions of comorbidities, lab values, and medication orders; and (3) standardized clinical vignettes aligned with common chart-review tasks. No new prospective clinician recruitment was required.

\textbf{Case selection criteria:}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Complex longitudinal clinical cases with multi-domain content (notes, labs, medications, imaging reports)
  \item Tasks requiring at least three topic pivots to reflect realistic information-seeking behavior
  \item Publicly available or synthetic data sources that are standard in clinical retrieval research
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Benchmark Case Selection}
Based on prior work on EHR retrieval with a baseline median time-to-correct-information of approximately 120 seconds (SD 60 seconds), we selected a case set designed to detect a 20\% improvement in task completion time. The benchmark includes 60 complex chart-review tasks with sufficient variance in topic shifts, yielding 480 evaluation points across conditions. This case selection supports comparison of CMA against standard search under controlled conditions.

\subsection{Randomization and Blinding}
Task order was randomized using computer-generated block randomization (blocks of 4) to control for order effects. Outcome adjudicators scoring task correctness and accuracy were blinded to condition assignment via review of de-identified logs and task identifiers.

\subsection{Intervention Description}

\textbf{CMA-augmented EHR retrieval (intervention):} The CMA system was integrated into a custom EHR search interface with the following components:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Session intent encoder:} Transformed user search history and queries into SPD matrix representations of active session intent.
  \item \textbf{Curvature-Aware Interference (CAI) gate:} Continuously monitored the curvature of the intent trajectory. When curvature exceeded a learned threshold (indicating an abrupt topic shift), the gate attenuated contributions of prior queries to the current retrieval score, suppressing stale context and improving relevance after pivots.
  \item \textbf{JEPA predictor:} Forecasted the next 1--2 steps of user intent in latent embedding space, enabling prefetch of likely-relevant documents. Top-k prefetched results (k=5--10) were surfaced in a right-side panel labeled ``Suggested items based on your search trajectory'' with confidence scores and source provenance.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Control (standard session-based search):} Baseline EHR search with naive session context management. Prior queries were retained via a fixed recency-weighted history window (last 10 queries, exponentially downweighted). No curvature-aware gating or predictive prefetch. Identical UI except for absence of proactive suggestions panel.

\subsection{Data Collection Procedures}

For each vignette task, the following were recorded:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Task start/end timestamps
  \item All search queries typed and their timestamps
  \item Timestamp and identity of the first correct piece of information retrieved (as determined by a blinded adjudicator using a pre-defined correctness rubric)
  \item Final answer submitted for each task
  \item All system interaction events with sub-millisecond timing
  \item System retrieval latency for each query (ms)
  \item System logs of prefetched items, CAI gate activation, and JEPA predictions
  \item Screen recordings (video and audio, consented)
\end{itemize}

A separate expert annotation review instrument collected perceived usefulness, trust, and safety assessments for the CMA condition and control condition.

All logs were stored on secure, HIPAA-compliant research servers with role-based access controls.

\subsection{Outcome Measures}

\textbf{Primary outcomes:}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Time-to-correct-information (seconds):} Elapsed time from task start to first retrieval of information that meets the correctness rubric. Right-censored at 300 seconds if not achieved.
  \item \textbf{Retrieval accuracy (\%):} Proportion of tasks in which correct information was retrieved within the 300-second task window.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Secondary outcomes:}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Cognitive load (NASA-TLX score):} Composite load across mental demand, physical demand, temporal demand, performance, effort, and frustration (0--100 scale, higher = greater load).
  \item \textbf{System usability (SUS score):} Standardized 10-item usability instrument (0--100 scale).
  \item \textbf{Perceived usefulness:} 5-point Likert response to ``The system helped me find relevant information efficiently.''
  \item \textbf{System latency (median ms per query):} Average retrieval latency across all queries in a session.
  \item \textbf{Error rate and analysis:} Proportion and nature of incorrect actions (e.g., selecting irrelevant suggestions, premature answer submission) that may be attributable to CMA suggestions vs. baseline performance. Blinded adjudicators will review any contested cases.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

\textbf{Primary analysis:} Time-to-correct-information was analyzed using a linear mixed-effects model with the specification:

\begin{equation}
\log(Y_{ij}) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{Condition}_{ij} + \beta_2 \text{Period}_{ij} + \beta_3 \text{VignetteType}_{ij} + b_i + \epsilon_{ij}
\end{equation}

where $Y_{ij}$ is time-to-correct-information for task $i$ in condition $j$, $\beta_0$ is the intercept, $\beta_1$ is the condition effect (CMA vs control), $\beta_2$ and $\beta_3$ are period and vignette-type effects, $b_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_b^2)$ is the random intercept for task $i$, and $\epsilon_{ij} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_e^2)$ is the residual error. Data were log-transformed due to right-skew; results are reported as percent change via $\% \text{ change} = (e^{\beta_1} - 1) \times 100\%$.

Retrieval accuracy was analyzed using mixed-effects logistic regression:

\begin{equation}
\text{logit}(P(\text{Accuracy}_{ij} = 1)) = \alpha_0 + \alpha_1 \text{Condition}_{ij} + \alpha_2 \text{Period}_{ij} + \alpha_3 \text{VignetteType}_{ij} + u_i
\end{equation}

where $u_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_u^2)$ is the random intercept for task $i$.

Effect sizes (Cohen's d for continuous outcomes) were reported with 95\% confidence intervals. Significance threshold: $\alpha = 0.05$ (uncorrected, primary outcomes pre-specified).

\textbf{Secondary analyses:} Cognitive load (NASA-TLX), SUS, and latency were analyzed similarly. Subgroup analyses explored heterogeneity by specialty, years of experience, and vignette complexity via interaction terms.

\textbf{Missing data and sensitivity:} No significant task-level missingness occurred. Benchmark data were complete across both conditions.

\textbf{Reporting:} This evaluation followed reporting guidance for computational clinical retrieval studies. All pre-specified analyses are reported below.

\subsection{Data Provenance and Privacy}
The evaluation used publicly available de-identified datasets and synthetic cases, ensuring no new patient-protected information was introduced. All data sources were handled according to established privacy and data-use agreements. System logs and benchmark results were stored on secure, HIPAA-compliant research servers with role-based access controls. De-identified datasets and evaluation code will be shared upon publication via Open Science Framework.

\subsection{Reporting Guidelines}
This manuscript follows reporting guidance for computational evaluation of clinical retrieval systems and simulation-based clinical informatics research.



\section{Results}

\subsection{Benchmark Case Selection and Data Characteristics}
Sixty complex chart-review cases were selected from publicly available and synthetic clinical datasets to evaluate CMA under realistic information-seeking conditions. Cases included multi-domain EHR content (notes, medications, labs, imaging reports) and were chosen to require at least three topic pivots per task. The benchmark yielded 480 total evaluation tasks across CMA and control conditions.

