\end{figure}

\textbf{Retrieval accuracy:} CMA maintained high and non-inferior retrieval accuracy compared to standard search. Across all 480 benchmark tasks, correct information was retrieved in 89.2\% of CMA tasks (256/288 tasks) versus 87.5\% of control tasks (252/288 tasks). Mixed-effects logistic regression showed no significant difference between conditions (odds ratio 1.14, 95\% CI: 0.76--1.71, p=0.24), confirming non-inferiority of CMA (margin 5\% pre-specified).

The time-to-correct-information distribution (Figure~\ref{fig:time_distribution}) illustrates the rightward shift in the control condition, confirming longer task completion times despite equivalent accuracy.

\subsection{Secondary Outcomes}

\textbf{Cognitive load (NASA-TLX):} CMA significantly reduced subjective cognitive load. Mean NASA-TLX composite score was 52.3 (SD 14.1) in the CMA condition versus 61.8 (SD 15.4) in the control condition (difference --9.5, 95\% CI: --14.2 to --4.8, p=0.001). This 15.4\% reduction represents a substantial decrease in perceived mental demand, temporal pressure, and effort (Figure~\ref{fig:tlx}). Subscale analysis showed reductions across all six TLX domains (mental demand, physical demand, temporal demand, performance, effort, frustration), with largest reductions in temporal demand (--2.8 points, p=0.002) and effort (--2.1 points, p=0.004).

