  \label{fig:tlx}
\end{figure}

\textbf{System usability (SUS):} System Usability Scale scores were comparable between conditions (CMA 74.2, SD 11.3 vs Control 71.8, SD 12.6, p=0.18), with both well above the 68-point acceptability threshold, indicating good overall usability of both interfaces.

\textbf{Perceived usefulness:} Expert reviewers rated the CMA system as significantly more useful for finding relevant information. Mean Likert response (1--5 scale, 5=strongly agree) was 4.21 (SD 0.68) for CMA versus 3.56 (SD 0.82) for control (p<0.001), indicating the system was judged more effective at supporting efficient retrieval.

\textbf{System latency:} CMA achieved substantial reductions in mean retrieval latency (Figure~\ref{fig:latency}). Median latency per query was 145 milliseconds (IQR 120--178) in the CMA condition versus 188 milliseconds (IQR 158--219) in the control condition (p<0.001), a 23.1\% reduction. This improvement aligns with JEPA-driven prefetching reducing cold-start retrieval penalties.

