  \caption{Median retrieval latency per query. CMA achieved 23.1\% reduction (145 ms vs 188 ms, p<0.001) via JEPA-driven prefetching, reducing cold-start retrieval penalty and supporting rapid decision-making in time-critical clinical scenarios.}
  \label{fig:latency}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Error and safety analysis:} No action sequences were adjudicated as attributable errors introduced by CMA suggestions. In 4 cases, a proactive suggestion proved incorrect; however, these were classified as acceptable exploration rather than errors, as the system or reviewer subsequently corrected the retrieval path. No adverse or clinically concerning erroneous retrievals were observed. The proportion of tasks with confidence-low outcomes did not differ between conditions (CMA 8.7\% vs Control 9.2\%, p=0.71).

\subsection{Subgroup Analyses}

Figure~\ref{fig:subgroup} presents forest plot of subgroup analyses. \textbf{By specialty:} Time-to-correct-information improvements from CMA were consistent across specialties. Emergency medicine showed the largest benefit (22.1\% reduction, 95\% CI 8.5\%--34.2\%), followed by critical care (19.3\%), hospital medicine (17.8\%), and internal medicine (14.9\%), with no significant heterogeneity (p=0.18).

