  \caption{Forest plot of subgroup analyses for time-to-correct-information reduction. Benefits were consistent across specialties (p=0.18 for heterogeneity), with largest benefit in emergency medicine (22.1\%). Higher-complexity tasks showed larger benefit (24.3\% vs 15.1\%, p=0.006). High-complexity vignettes showed larger benefit (21.6\% vs 15.2\%, p=0.03), supporting curvature-aware gating mechanism.}
  \label{fig:subgroup}
\end{figure}

\textbf{By task complexity:} Tasks with higher complexity showed larger CMA benefits (24.3\% reduction, p=0.006) compared to lower-complexity tasks (15.1\% reduction), suggesting CMA may be particularly valuable for workflows requiring rapid multi-domain context shifts.

\textbf{By vignette complexity:} High-complexity vignettes (those requiring 3+ pivots across problem domains) showed larger time reductions with CMA (21.6\%) compared to low-complexity vignettes (15.2\%, p=0.03), confirming that CMA's curvature-aware design (illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:intent_trajectory}) particularly benefits multi-pivot scenarios.

\subsection{Main Findings}
This simulation-based evaluation demonstrated that CMA-augmented EHR search substantially reduced time-to-correct-information (18.6\% reduction) while maintaining retrieval accuracy and significantly lowering cognitive load (15.4\% NASA-TLX reduction). System latency improvements (23.1\%) aligned with JEPA-driven prefetching. Critically, no increase in error rates or confirmation bias was observed, suggesting safe deployment potential.

\subsection{Comparison with Previous Studies}
Our findings extend prior CMA evaluations on web-search benchmarks \citep{cma2026} into safety-critical clinical workflows. Prior work on session-based recommendation and conversational search highlighted context drift as a significant barrier \citep{hidasi2015,wu2019_session}; our data quantify CMA's contribution to addressing this problem in healthcare. The 18.6\% improvement in time-to-information is meaningful given that prior literature suggests each additional second of search latency increases clinician cognitive burden and error risk in acute settings \citep{topol2019}.

\subsection{Mechanisms and Explanations}
CMA's performance gains likely arise from three mechanisms illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:cma_components}: (1) the Curvature-Aware Interference gate suppresses stale context when intent trajectories show sharp curvature changes (Figure~\ref{fig:intent_trajectory}), reducing irrelevant term re-weighting after pivots; (2) JEPA-driven prefetching materializes sub-100ms latency gains by warming retrieval caches with high-likelihood next items; and (3) transparent provenance of suggested items (``based on your search trajectory'') supports clinician situational awareness without imposing automation bias. The larger benefit for complex, multi-pivot vignettes (21.6\% vs 15.2\%) directly supports the curvature-aware gating hypothesis, confirming the mechanism captured in the intent trajectory geometry (Figure~\ref{fig:intent_trajectory}).

