\subsection{Strengths}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Rigorous randomized crossover benchmark design with complete task data and no missing values.
  \item Blinded outcome assessment via de-identified logs, reducing performance bias.
  \item Comprehensive outcome measurement including time-to-information, accuracy, cognitive load, usability, and latency.
  \item Subgroup analyses demonstrating consistent benefits across specialties and exploring effect heterogeneity.
  \item Reporting aligned with clinical informatics evaluation guidance.
  \item Use of realistic MIMIC-derived synthetic data and well-validated outcome measures (NASA-TLX, SUS).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Simulation setting with synthetic charts may not capture real-world EHR interruptions, alert fatigue, or clinical time pressure.
  \item Eight vignettes per session, while sufficient for within-subject comparisons, may not represent the full spectrum of clinical searching.
  \item Partial inability to blind expert reviewers to UI differences may introduce assessment bias despite de-identified logging.
  \item Single benchmarking environment limits generalizability to other institutions with different EHR systems and workflows.
  \item Proactive suggestions in clinical contexts require vigilance to prevent over-reliance or confirmation bias; longer-term real-world deployment studies are needed.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Clinical and Operational Implications}
If validated in real-world EHR deployment, CMA could materially improve clinician efficiency during chart review and support rapid decision-making in acute care. Key implementation considerations: (1) maintain transparent provenance for all suggestions; (2) design override and opt-out mechanisms prominently; (3) monitor for confirmation bias through audit logs and adverse event surveillance; (4) conduct human factors studies on trust calibration and clinician decision-making processes. CMA should be positioned as a decision-support aid rather than autonomous agent.

\subsection{Future Research}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Multi-center real-world deployment studies with integration into live EHR systems and measurement of downstream clinical outcomes (e.g., diagnostic accuracy, time-to-treatment).
  \item Investigation of long-term effects on clinician behavior and trust calibration over weeks to months of use.
  \item Evaluation of CMA's performance on rare or complex diagnostic scenarios not well-represented in training data.
  \item Human factors and qualitative studies on clinician mental models and over-reliance risks.
  \item Extension to proactive alerts and order suggestions beyond search.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

This randomized benchmark evaluation demonstrates that the Continuum Memory Architecture successfully reduces time-to-information and cognitive burden in simulated EHR search tasks without sacrificing accuracy. CMA's curvature-aware suppression of stale context and JEPA-driven predictive prefetching appear safe and effective in controlled settings. With continued safeguards to maintain oversight and transparency, CMA warrants investigation in real-world clinical deployment. Future work should focus on multi-center trials, evaluation of diagnostic outcomes, and human factors research to ensure responsible implementation.

